% =======================================================
\section{Initialization and Reference Alignment}
\label{sec:init_alignment}
\label{sec:startup-policy}

The deployed startup keeps attitude initialization outside the MEKF.  The same
measurement-only Mahony observer used by the adaptation front end
(Sec.~\ref{sec:vertical-proxy}) runs from the first sample, while the MEKF state
is left untouched.  In parallel, the wave-period and acceleration-variance
estimators converge toward the initial operating point.  When attitude,
magnetic reference, and tuner quality are adequate, the completed proxy
solution is handed to the MEKF and the filter starts directly in its normal
Live configuration.  The article therefore does not treat the older degraded
MEKF warmup path as part of the deployed algorithm.

\subsection{Tilt and operating-point bootstrap}

Before magnetic north is established, yaw is a gauge degree of freedom.  The
proxy is initialized from accelerometer direction, which constrains roll and
pitch but leaves yaw arbitrary, and then evolves according to
Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony}.  Its low correction bandwidth prevents ordinary
orbital acceleration from being interpreted as rapid tilt, while the integral
term estimates the standing gyro bias that would otherwise survive in an
attitude seed.

The adaptation front end runs independently at the same time.  It estimates the
wave-band period and acceleration level and stages the corresponding
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$ operating point.  These writes are model parameters,
not state corrections, so the first propagated MEKF sample already uses the
best available sea-state schedule instead of starting from the nominal period
and adapting after handoff.

\subsection{Quality-gated handoff}
\label{sec:proxy-handoff}

Normal handoff requires three conditions simultaneously: (i) proxy tilt has
held gravity agreement for \SI{2}{s}, with sine-angle error below \num{0.075}
(about \SI{4.3}{\degree}) and a veto while gyro magnitude exceeds
\SI{30}{\degree\per\second}; (ii) magnetic north has been gauged when a
magnetometer is present; and (iii) the sea-state tuner is ready.  A minimum
startup duration of \SI{8}{s} prevents an accidentally quiet initial interval
from passing the gate before the observer has integrated useful information.
A bounded timeout of \SI{150}{s} guarantees that a difficult sea cannot keep
the estimator outside Live indefinitely; the timeout is internally constrained
not to interrupt an active magnetic-reference acquisition.

At handoff, the MEKF attitude is seeded once from the proxy solution and its
attitude covariance is anisotropic,
\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{tilt}}=\SI{0.035}{rad},\qquad
\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\SI{0.087}{rad},
\]
when north has been established, while yaw receives the much looser
$\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\pi/2$ when no magnetic gauge is available.  The linear
$[v,p,S,a_w]$ block, nominal accelerometer covariance, current OU parameters,
and integral-state regularizer are enabled together.  Accelerometer-bias
learning remains externally held until the magnetic refinement described below
has landed, because bias and small tilt error are weakly separable in waves and
the residual bias has a very long correlation time.

This handoff is a hard initialization event.  It is deliberately outside the
Live-state ISS theorem of Sec.~\ref{app:iss-stability}; the theorem begins after
the state and covariance have been seeded and no longer treats startup as a
sequence of ordinary Kalman updates.

\subsection{Two-stage magnetic reference}
\label{sec:mag-two-stage}

Magnetic reference acquisition uses the proxy tilt rather than the MEKF.  Raw
magnetometer samples are accumulated in a yaw-stripped level frame, so the
proxy's arbitrary yaw cannot contaminate the learned field direction.  The
averaging window is specified in seconds rather than samples and spans multiple
wave periods, reducing wave-correlated levelling error.

The deployed acquisition has two stages because fast availability and accurate
reference formation have conflicting time scales.  A provisional reference is
accepted once the gravity gate permits it, typically providing a heading and
MEKF handoff within roughly \SIrange{22}{52}{s} in the scored simulations.  A
second \SI{30}{s} acquisition beginning at \SI{90}{s} then relearns the
reference after the low-bandwidth proxy has settled.  The refinement remains
framed on the proxy: using the already-live MEKF would make the new reference
self-confirm the provisional reference it is intended to replace.

When the refined reference is accepted, the world magnetic reference is
replaced and yaw is re-gauged once.  Accelerometer-bias learning is released
only after this event.  The refinement occurs before the common
\SI{900}{s} scoring window, but it is still a discrete reconfiguration and is
excluded from the continuous Live interval covered by the stability theorem.

The two-stage procedure is an engineering compromise, not an observability
proof.  A longer initial wait can produce a cleaner first reference but delays
usable heading; an earlier reference increases the burden on the later
refinement.  The reported default fixes that trade for the evaluated device and
sea family rather than claiming a universal startup schedule.

\subsection{Live re-lock boundary}

A sufficiently large sustained tilt inconsistency can trigger a tilt-only
re-lock that preserves the current yaw gauge.  The deployed safeguard requires
a tilt inconsistency above $70^\circ$ for \SI{0.35}{s}, followed by a
\SI{3}{s} cooldown before another re-lock is permitted.  Such resets are safety
behavior, not part of the nominal Kalman recursion.  They are therefore treated
exactly like startup and magnetic re-gauging in the analysis: bounded and
testable in implementation, but outside the local ISS theorem between reset
events.
